<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges de La Tour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1593–1652</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges de La Tour was a painter from the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who worked mainly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>. He is known for religious and genre scenes, many lit by a single candle, influenced by the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, and he received the title of painter in ordinary to Louis XIII. Forgotten after his death, his work was rediscovered in the early twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-de-la-tour.jpg" alt="Georges de La Tour" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges de La Tour · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
